<SubSection title="Convective Heat Transfer Coefficient" id="HeatTransferCoeff"> 
<p>To solve for the convective, heat transfer coefficient (<InlineEquation equation="h"/>):</p>
<Enumerate>
<Item> Identify the solid's geometry: flat plate, cylinder, or sphere</Item>
<Item> Find <InlineEquation equation="L"/> (characteristic length) </Item>
<Item> Identify natural or forced convection</Item>
<Item> Identify laminar or turbulent flow using the Reynold's Number</Item>
<Item> Choose the appropriate equation</Item>
</Enumerate>

<p> To find the various Nusselt Number equations based on geometry and type of convection, 
visit <i><a href="https://volupe.com/support/empirical-correlations-for-convective-heat-transfer-coefficients/" target="_blank">Empirical Correlations for Convective Heat Transfer Coefficients</a></i>.</p>

<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
    <CalloutCard title="Heads Up!">
     <p> When considering convection over a surface, the characteristic length refers to <i>how far the fluid must travel over/around the system</i>. 
		For example, fluid encountering a sphere would need to travel around its diameter.</p>
	  <p> This differs from the characteristic length described in <a href="/transfer/Conduction/#CharacteristicLength">conduction</a>, which is the <i>path that heat must take to escape from a solid object</i>.
			In this instance, heat would escape most quickly through the radius of the sphere.</p>
    </CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>

<p><b>Flat Plate, Forced Convection</b></p>
<p>The convective heat transfer coefficient,  <InlineEquation equation="h"/>, varies with distance along the flow for both laminar and turbulent flow. </p>

<p> To calculate <InlineEquation equation="h_{x}"/> at a specific location <InlineEquation equation="x"/>, use the local Nusselt number (<InlineEquation equation="Nu_{x}"/>): <p>
<DisplayEquation equation="Nu_{x} = \\frac{h_{x}x}{k_{fluid}}" title="" background="True"/>

<p><p>To calculate <InlineEquation equation="h_{L}"/> over the entire object (<InlineEquation equation="L"/>), use the average Nusselt number (<InlineEquation equation="Nu_{L}"/>): <p>
<DisplayEquation equation="Nu_{L} = \\frac{h_{L}L}{k_{fluid}}" title="" background="True"/>

<p><b>Flat Plate, Natural Convection</b></p>
<p> In conditions of natural convection, fluid moves due to changes in density compared to its surroundings (such as changes in temperature). 
With no forced convection, the Reynolds Number is not as useful. Instead, the Grashof Number can approximate the ratio of the buoyancy to viscous force on the fluid.</p>

<p><b>Cylinder, Forced Convection</b></p>
<Itemize>
<Item> Flow over the cylinder: characteristic length = outside diameter <InlineEquation equation="D_{o}"/></Item>
<Item> Flow through the cylinder: characteristic length = inner diameter <InlineEquation equation="D_{i}"/> of the tube</Item>
</Itemize>

<p><b>Cylinder, Natural Convection</b></p>
<Itemize>
<Item> Vertical cylinder: characteristic length = height <InlineEquation equation="L"/> of cylinder</Item>
<Item> Horizontal cylinder: characteristic length = outside diameter <InlineEquation equation="D_{o}"/> of cylinder</Item>
</Itemize>

<p><b>Sphere</b></p>
<p>The characteristic length for both forced and natural convection is the diameter <InlineEquation equation="D"/> of the sphere.<p>
</SubSection>




